GeoMart Integration – Java Code Walkthrough
1	Java class structure and initialization
1.1	Class and dependencies (lines 1–65)
GeoMartIntegrationConfigExecutor is a custom Java class that works with SailPoint IdentityIQ. It uses SailPoint objects to process provisioning requests, the Web Services connector to read accounts, and an HTTP client to call ArcGIS APIs. Separate helper classes manage users, roles, groups and tags. The class also refers to a GeoMart-specific email template.
1.2	configure() (lines 187–192)
This method saves the SailPoint context and calls the parent class setup method. It does not select the target application here. That happens when the provisioning request is processed.
1.3	Application selection (around line 210)
When an account request arrives, the code reads the application name from that request and loads the matching application in IIQ. This means the same executor can potentially work with different ArcGIS applications, although the helper code and settings still need to be checked.
2	Method-by-method walkthrough
2.1	updateRequestStatus() (approximately lines 72–159)
This method checks the status of an identity request against what is actually present on the ArcGIS account. It looks up the request, reads the account and compares the requested license with the license assigned to the user. We noticed a condition involving the request ID that may not handle null values correctly, so it needs a code review.
2.2	aggregateSingleAccount() (lines 162–183)
This method uses the Web Services connector to fetch one ArcGIS account by its username or native identity. It is used to check whether a user already exists and to confirm account changes. It depends on the application’s Get Object operation being configured correctly.
2.3	provision() (lines 195–255)
This is the main entry point for provisioning. It reads the account requests, identifies the target application and sends Create requests to create() and Modify requests to modify(). It also records success, failure or retry status. Error handling should be tested, especially when a plan contains more than one request.
2.4	create() (lines 426–540)
First, the method checks whether the account already exists. If it does, the request is marked successful without checking whether the existing account has the right license or role. For a new user, it prepares the account details, adds the license and mapped role, and calls the ArcGIS createUser API. After a successful response, it reads the account again and updates extra tags such as manager, department and business unit. So account creation involves several steps, not just one API call.
2.5	modify() (lines 257–423)
This method handles license changes and user tag updates. When a license is added, it may first enable a disabled account, compare the current license and then update the license and role. Some changes also require group membership or content ownership updates. When a license is removed, the code may disable the account if no Add operation was processed. That does not necessarily mean the user has no remaining access, so this behavior needs careful testing.
2.6	getToken() (lines 543–569)
This method reads the ArcGIS token stored in the application settings. When it decides the token needs refreshing, it runs the connector’s Test Connection operation and reads the new token. The expiry calculation appears to refresh later than intended, so token timing and missing expiry values should be validated. Tokens should not appear in logs.
2.7	getBaseUrl() and getUrlForCreate() (lines 572–588)
getBaseUrl() reads the REST base URL from the selected IIQ application. getUrlForCreate() builds the ArcGIS user creation URL using the Portal hostname and token. We should avoid exposing the token in logs or unnecessary URL strings.
2.8	getRoleFromLicense() (lines 609–620)
This method finds the Portal role associated with the requested license using application settings. If no mapping is found, it uses a default role and then a hardcoded fallback. For ELEVATE, the team should approve each license-to-role mapping and decide what should happen if a mapping is missing.
2.9	enableAccount() and disableAccount() (lines 622–684)
These methods do not call the ArcGIS APIs directly. They create new SailPoint provisioning requests and let the IIQ Provisioner run the configured Web Services Enable or Disable operation. Enable checks the account again afterward; Disable appears to return success without the same verification.
2.10	generateRequestId() (lines 592–606)
This method reads the identity request ID from the provisioning plan. The ID helps connect provisioning activity back to the original SailPoint request. We should also check what happens when provisioning runs without an identity request ID.


